<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  The monthly payment is the number a sales desk wants you to negotiate, because almost any price can be made into a payment you recognize. The contract you sign is a different set of numbers: the amount financed, the annual percentage rate, the term in months, and the finance charge. This hub is about reading those four before you agree to the payment, and about where a refinance fits later if the first loan was the one you could get that day rather than the one you should keep.
</p>

<h2>Price the car, then price the money</h2>
<p>
  A cash price and a financed price are allowed to differ, and they often do, because a manufacturer incentive may require you to use the captive lender. That can be a good trade. It can also be a rebate that costs more in interest than it saved on the sticker. You only see which one it is if the purchase price is written down before the lender is chosen. Settle the vehicle price, including fees you cannot avoid (tax, title, registration) and excluding products you can (a service contract, gap, paint, etch). Then compare lenders against that amount.
</p>
<p>
  The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau’s auto-loan materials, including what to look for in an offer, are at <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/auto-loans/" rel="noopener">consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/auto-loans</a>. Our snapshot of how rates differ by credit and by new versus used is <a href="/guides/financing/auto-loan-rates/">auto loan rates</a>. Those pages describe the market. They are not a quote. Your rate is the one a lender puts in writing for this VIN, this term, and your credit.
</p>

<h2>APR, term, and the amount financed</h2>
<p>
  APR is the cost of credit as a yearly rate, including certain finance charges. It is the number to compare across offers of the same term and the same amount. A lower monthly payment produced by stretching the term from 60 months to 84 months is not a lower cost. You pay more interest, and you spend more months owing more than the car is worth, which matters if the car is totaled or if you need to sell.
</p>
<p>
  The amount financed is the price minus your down payment and any real trade equity, plus anything the lender agreed to roll in. Negative equity from the last car, a service contract, and taxes can all land in that number. Each one is a choice. Negative equity in particular means you are borrowing to retire the last loan, and the new car’s value does not secure that extra principal. If a lender will not finance it, that is information, not an insult.
</p>
<p>
  A down payment reduces the amount financed and, at many lenders, the rate tier you land in. It also gives you room if you have to sell sooner than the loan amortizes. There is no universal “right” percentage. There is the amount that keeps the payment and the term inside what you can pay off without stretching the car past the point you wanted to own it.
</p>

<h2>Where to get the quote</h2>
<p>
  Credit unions, banks, online lenders, and the dealer’s captive finance company are all real options. Take at least one written offer from outside the dealership into the store. A dealer that can beat it will, and a dealer that cannot will stop describing its rate as the special one. Pre-qualification, when the lender offers it, is usually a soft look at your credit and a rate that is still subject to the car and to a final application. The hard inquiry happens when you actually apply. Several inquiries for the same kind of loan in a short window are generally treated as one shopping event by credit-scoring models, but the window is not infinite. Do the shopping in days, not in months.
</p>
<p>
  Read the contract for a prepayment penalty. Most auto loans do not have one. If this one does, it belongs in the comparison, because the whole point of a later refinance is the right to pay the first loan off. Also read whether the rate is fixed. A variable rate on a car loan is unusual and worth questioning.
</p>
<p>
  Dealer reserve, the markup a dealer may add to a lender’s buy rate, is one reason the same credit profile sees two rates on two lots. You will not always be shown the buy rate. An outside offer is how you detect the markup without needing the dealer’s worksheet.
</p>

<h2>The car still has to be the car</h2>
<p>
  Lenders finance a specific vehicle. A VIN that does not match the title, a branded title the lender will not take, or an undisclosed salvage history can unwind the deal after you think you have a rate. Decode the VIN and check the title before you let anyone submit an application that names that car. The <a href="/guides/buying/">buying hub</a> is the sequence. A loan on the wrong car is not a cheap loan.
</p>
<p>
  Gap coverage is the difference between what you owe and what the insurer pays if the car is a total loss. It is useful when you owe more than the car is worth, which is common on long terms and on loans that rolled in negative equity. It is a poor buy when you put enough down that the loan starts below the car’s value. Price it from your insurer as well as from the finance office. The two products are not always the same contract.
</p>

<h2>Refinancing later</h2>
<p>
  A refinance replaces the loan. It makes sense when your credit has improved, when rates available to you have fallen, or when you want a shorter term and can afford the higher payment. It does not make sense when fees and any prepaid add-ons wipe out the interest you would save, or when you stretch the term again and restart the clock. Ask the new lender for the finance charge on the remaining balance and compare it with the finance charge left on the current loan, not with the original payment.
</p>
<p>
  You will need the payoff letter from the current lender, the VIN, and proof of insurance. The same title issues that blocked the first loan will block the second. If the car has an open recall, refinancing does not clear it. Schedule the free dealer repair anyway. That obligation is the manufacturer’s, described by NHTSA at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>, and it has nothing to do with who holds the lien.
</p>

<h2>What we will not tell you</h2>
<p>
  We will not tell you that a particular credit score “gets” a particular rate, or that refinancing always saves a fixed percent. Both claims are marketing. The rate is the one in the Truth in Lending disclosure on your contract. If a payment quote does not come with that disclosure, you do not have an offer yet. You have a conversation.
</p>

